a new film documents a sky news  investigation  and a 40-year battle by campaigners  against  pharmaceutical giant all about a pill  called primidos  used as a pregnancy test in the 1960s  and 70s  which parents believed caused  malformations to their babies in the  womb  an untold part of that story is the role  of a former prime minister does the  prime minister agree with me that  those families do now deserve justice  and there should be a chance to launch a  public inquiry into this terrible  scandal  prime minister my honourable friend has  raised an important issue  theresa may called for a review into  primitos after pressure from her own mps  and after reading a scientific review in  2017 which had found there wasn't a link  between the drug and malformations but  she felt  didn't make a convincing case certainly  when i looked at the report i felt that  it wasn't  the slam dunk answer that people had had  said it was  i explained to within my number 10 team  that i think that we should look at this  we should have a review on this  ultimately if the prime minister decides  it should go ahead then it will go ahead  did you get any pushback well i don't  think  those around the the healthcare system  were too pleased at the idea that there  was going to be some further work on  this  and on other issues because for many  years  they had been um saying no there's no  problem  one almost felt it was sort of women  being patted on the head and said  they're there dear don't worry you know  you're imagining it or it's it's not you  don't know we know better than you do  in july the cumberland review team found  that there was  avoidable harm caused by a failure to  regulate primitives  their report said victims should get a  government apology  and funding from the state and  manufacturer  reading it left lead campaigner mary  lyon almost speechless  that's more than expected  it's incredible that somebody that like  murray lyon  and other campaigners have held fast to  this campaign  despite all the many uh potential  setbacks they've had in terms of  of the government and and healthcare  professionals trying to stop them in  their tracks  well when i became prime minister the  first speech i gave on the steps of  number 10  was about social justice and the need to  deal with burning injustices  and i think the way that the campaigners  on primados  were treated was an injustice i think  it's important that the government looks  at the whole question of redress and  about how that redress  can be brought for four people  they've had an apology and that's  important but obviously lives have  suffered as a result  the manufacturer of primitives doesn't  feel responsible  shearing is now owned by bail which  insists the available evidence doesn't  support an association  between the drug and malformations back  in july the cumberland review team said  that bayer had a moral  and ethical duty to make amends to the  victims of primitos  now we've approached the company's chief  executive for an interview and he's been  unavailable  so we've come here to their headquarters  in leverkusen in germany  to see if he'll speak to us mr bauman  from sky news are you going to  compensate the victims of primitives  is bear going to pay them compensation  would you have any message to the  manufacturer  well i think they need to perhaps to  look at the history of this  at the time that primados was still  being prescribed  and people were still taking primos that  there was  a known risk a known potential risk  and ask themselves whether it was right  for that to have carried on  this prime minister will be remembered  for troubled brexit negotiations and  internal party battles over the eu but  she'll hope this and other inquiries  into hillsborough child sexual abuse and  contaminated blood  will also be part of her legacy jason  farrell sky news
 

